WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.365 --> 00:00:03.784
[CHIME RINGING]

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:07.680
JEN HECHT: Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.780
I'm Jen Hecht and I'm the VP of
People Operations at RStudio.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.910
And I'm thrilled to
be on this stage today

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:18.780
with some folks who are going
to help us talk about building

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.470
a career in data science, in
particular around the use of R,

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.810
but not exclusively R.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:29.100
So let me just introduce the
panelists that we have today.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.189
Sitting right next to me,
we have Sydeaka Watson.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:35.280
She is the founder at
Korelasi Data Insights

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.600
and also senior data
scientist at Elicit Insights.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.880
We have David Keyes,
who's a founder

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.010
of R for the Rest of Us.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:48.120
And we also have Gabi de
Queiroz, senior engineering

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.450
and data science manager
at IBM, and also founder

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.750
at AI Inclusive and R-Ladies.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.160
So, welcome.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:56.660
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:57.606
Yeah.

00:00:57.606 --> 00:00:58.552
Very cool.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.010
So I thought we'd get
started by asking each of you

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:08.160
to talk a little bit about
your own experiences sort

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.880
of growing your career
in data science.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.010
We've heard, I think,
a few panelists today,

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.260
or speakers today,
talk about what

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:17.760
is it to be a data scientist.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.960
I just heard a talk
about salary structures.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.460
And I think it's a topic that's
relevant for a lot of people

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.390
in different ways.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.470
I'm interested in hearing
a little bit about how

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:31.750
did each of you experience
milestones in your data science

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:32.250
career?

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.280
Or perhaps if you're a
team manager or somebody

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.540
working with people that are
learning as data science, what

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.408
are the stages of this and how
have you moved through them?

00:01:44.408 --> 00:01:45.352
GABI DE QUEIROZ: Yeah.

00:01:45.352 --> 00:01:47.240
[Pause]

00:01:48.295 --> 00:01:49.128
GABI DE QUEIROZ: On.

00:01:49.128 --> 00:01:49.610
On.

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:50.600
JEN HECHT: All
right, we have a--

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:51.850
GABI DE QUEIROZ: Hello, hello?

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:52.970
Hello?

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:54.286
JEN HECHT: Can you hear Gabi?

00:01:54.286 --> 00:01:56.762
GABI DE QUEIROZ: Hello?

00:01:56.762 --> 00:01:57.262
OK.

00:01:57.262 --> 00:01:58.250
Try this one.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:01:58.750
Hello?

00:01:58.750 --> 00:01:59.742
No.

00:01:59.742 --> 00:02:00.740
OK.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:01.470
All right.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:01.970
All right.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:03.270
This one is working.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.370
Cool.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:08.539
So let me just summarize
kind of like my career path,

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:12.050
because it's interesting and
different from all of us here.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:16.460
So I was in academia
for about five years

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:20.370
doing research with
air pollution data.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:23.210
So then I moved to the industry.

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:26.690
So I had this transition
point where I'm like,

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:28.280
I want to try something else.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.380
I want to go to
work for a company.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:32.420
So then I became
a data scientist.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.450
So I was a statistician
before doing research

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:37.610
and then I became
a data scientist.

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:39.230
Also, I moved to San Francisco.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:41.870
So statisticians
in San Francisco,

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.610
they all became data scientists.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:48.110
So I was first
working for a startup

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:52.520
and then doing some
kind of like consulting.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.820
And then I moved to
a different startup,

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:59.030
and then became a
lead data scientist,

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:02.480
and then moved again, senior
data scientist, moved again,

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:05.270
became a developer
advocate, and then I

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:07.970
got to a point, an opportunity
to become a manager.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:13.482
So it's kind of like I don't
know exactly what are the--

00:03:13.482 --> 00:03:15.190
you wait two years
and then you did this.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:17.390
It's kind of like,
it's when I felt ready.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:19.100
And I think becoming
a manager was

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.620
a big jump from all
the other transitions

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:23.300
that I had to go through.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.940
So I felt like, I think I'm
ready to become a manager.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:27.063
Let's see how it goes.

00:03:27.063 --> 00:03:27.730
JEN HECHT: Yeah.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.190
OK, great.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:30.232
DAVID KEYES: [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:30.232 --> 00:03:30.710
SYDEAKA WATSON: Is it working?

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:31.188
Nope.

00:03:31.188 --> 00:03:32.144
DAVID KEYES: Try [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:32.144 --> 00:03:33.352
SYDEAKA WATSON: Hello, hello.

00:03:33.352 --> 00:03:35.020
OK, all right.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.170
Hi.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:39.350
So my path into data
science definitely

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:42.920
has not been
linear, particularly

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:48.950
because my motivations, my
just thoughts about who I was

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:52.040
and what I wanted to become
and the types of things

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.300
I wanted to do, they changed.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:57.350
So for example, when I was
in high school, I thought,

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:58.610
I love math.

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:01.215
The people that I see
around me in high school

00:04:01.215 --> 00:04:02.090
are my math teachers.

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:04.007
They love math, and they
became math teachers,

00:04:04.007 --> 00:04:06.290
and so I'll be a math teacher.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:07.790
And then at some
point, I discovered

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:10.610
that there were some other
things beyond education

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:12.260
that you could do
with math, and heard

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:16.279
about being a math
professor, and then found out

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:19.160
about biostatistics
after I became interested

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:20.600
in that type of career.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.690
So I ended up becoming a
biostatistics researcher.

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:27.860
And then as other people have
drank that same data science

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:31.400
Kool-Aid, heard about
all of the cool projects

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:34.460
and the great salary structures
associated with data science,

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.830
and decided that that was
something that I wanted to do.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:41.480
So some of it was just
exposure, where people told me

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:42.980
about other different
types of paths

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:45.950
that I might not have
thought about, and some of it

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:49.580
was just also just restlessness,
change, and interest.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.410
Maybe I was really
happy with my career

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:55.020
as a biostatistician
for the most part.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:57.407
I didn't have a problem with
my co-workers or the work.

00:04:57.407 --> 00:04:59.990
But it was just, I kind of want
to see what else is out there,

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:01.740
and I keep hearing
about this other thing,

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.868
so let me just try that.

00:05:03.868 --> 00:05:05.236
JEN HECHT: That's great.

00:05:05.236 --> 00:05:06.610
DAVID KEYES: I think this one--

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:07.420
can you hear me?

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:07.952
Yeah.

00:05:07.952 --> 00:05:09.190
Great.

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:14.685
So first of all, I should say,
I think my current work as well

00:05:14.685 --> 00:05:16.810
as my path is pretty
different from the other folks

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:18.580
on this stage.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:21.058
Unlike them, I work for myself.

00:05:21.058 --> 00:05:23.350
My business, as Jen mentioned,
is R for the Rest of Us.

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:26.260
And I primarily focus
on training folks

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:29.490
to use R. And I
particularly focus--

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:31.990
I think of it as kind of like
people who are scared about R,

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:33.430
but want to learn it.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.140
That's kind of my niche.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:40.163
And how I got into it,
it's very much by accident,

00:05:40.163 --> 00:05:42.580
which I know is a story that
resonates with a lot of folks

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:45.040
here.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:47.350
I'm not very
quantitatively trained.

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:49.580
I have a PhD in anthropology.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:52.870
My dissertation was
entirely qualitative.

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.310
Worked on some mixed methods
projects, kind of butted--

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:57.490
or not butted up against,
but came in contact

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:58.865
with some folks
who were using R.

00:05:58.865 --> 00:06:00.600
But never actually
used R in grad school.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:02.890
And it was actually
only when I started

00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:07.930
doing some consulting work
where I was doing what's

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:11.072
called program evaluation
work, looking--

00:06:11.072 --> 00:06:13.030
it was kind of applied
social science research,

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:16.150
looking at the impact of
programs run by non-profits,

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:17.553
that type of thing.

00:06:17.553 --> 00:06:18.970
And the organization
I was working

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:21.640
for allowed me to use
whatever software I wanted to.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:25.000
So I taught myself
R. And basically

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:26.710
since then, I
realized that there

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:29.470
was a need for other
people in that industry

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:33.370
as well as others to learn
R. And so I have kind of made

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:35.560
that my thing.

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:39.450
But now I forgot what your
original question was.

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:40.510
So if you want to--

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:42.207
JEN HECHT: Well,
we're free to divert

00:06:42.207 --> 00:06:43.290
from my original question.

00:06:45.803 --> 00:06:47.720
It's been interesting
to hear all the stories.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:50.290
I think one theme is
that it's non-linear.

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:53.960
It's not like there's a set
of predefined steps or ladder

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:57.453
or you got to do this before
you move on to the next thing.

00:06:57.453 --> 00:06:58.870
You hit on something
that when you

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:02.230
were talking about
having to teach yourself

00:07:02.230 --> 00:07:03.880
and not feeling like
you were already

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:07.610
part of a community that was
very quantitative in nature.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:12.220
And I think that's another
question I had for all of you.

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:14.812
How did you build that
kind of community,

00:07:14.812 --> 00:07:17.020
whether you were in industry,
when you were learning,

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:19.330
or you're coming out of
academia, you kind of go

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:20.590
out and find it for yourself?

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:22.797
I know all of you,
in your own ways,

00:07:22.797 --> 00:07:24.880
have been involved in
trying to bring other people

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:25.755
through that journey.

00:07:25.755 --> 00:07:27.580
But could you talk a
little bit about how

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:33.370
you started to find that for
yourself and how that evolved?

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:35.020
GABI DE QUEIROZ:
Yeah, so I did not

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:38.500
have this community
before back in Brazil.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:41.260
So when I moved over here
and created R-Ladies,

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.790
that was one of the intentions.

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:44.650
It's like, I don't
have a community,

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:46.600
so I'm going to create it.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:48.610
So that's how it worked for me.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:56.320
SYDEAKA WATSON: So I
could answer this question

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:00.220
in two different ways, according
to the period of my life

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:01.330
that it relates to.

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:02.950
So in the earlier
periods, of course,

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:06.790
where I'm new to the different
types of mathematical concepts

00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:07.978
and different statistical--

00:08:07.978 --> 00:08:09.520
I guess I didn't
mention this before.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:11.770
So my background is in
math and statistics.

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:14.890
So I have a bachelor's in
math, a master's in math,

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:17.255
a master's in statistics,
and a PhD in statistics,

00:08:17.255 --> 00:08:18.130
and no student loans.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.072
Yay.

00:08:19.072 --> 00:08:20.960
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:23.410
And so all of those
different types

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:26.470
of educational
experiences put me

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:28.270
into different types
of environments,

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:31.810
where I've got a
particular mix of students,

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:36.330
a particular set of faculty,
who are trying to, together,

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:39.400
help me to learn what I
need to learn from whatever

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.169
that perspective is.

00:08:41.169 --> 00:08:42.789
And so in some
sense, that community

00:08:42.789 --> 00:08:47.530
was curated for me just because
of where I was in school.

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:51.580
But one thing that I've
noticed as a professional

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:53.980
is that you don't always
have the benefit of having

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.470
those readily made communities.

00:08:56.470 --> 00:08:58.150
Sometimes you just
have to either create

00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:01.600
your own community, as
our esteemed panelist

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:02.680
did for R-Ladies.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:06.767
I'm also an R-Ladies Dallas
chapter organizer as well.

00:09:06.767 --> 00:09:08.600
So definitely understand
the value of those.

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:11.080
But sometimes you have to
be just very comfortable

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:16.870
being a community of size
one, and especially in places

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:19.180
where maybe you're the only
one who cares about data,

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:20.847
or you're the only
one who cares about R

00:09:20.847 --> 00:09:22.600
or whatever this
new model or tool is

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:27.730
or whatever, where you're
either internally motivated,

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:30.070
and you're just reading
and going online,

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:33.040
looking at blogs or different
types of user groups,

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:36.220
or you're also just going
out, finding meetup groups,

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:39.160
finding different types
of organizations, just

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:42.280
to connect with people who
have similar interests to you.

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:47.260
So I think it helps to develop
yourself internally and also

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:49.088
externally.

00:09:49.088 --> 00:09:51.380
DAVID KEYES: And for me, I
think the answer is actually

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:53.330
pretty straightforward.

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:55.220
Online communities, and
particularly Twitter,

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:59.840
have been really fundamental
in terms of my learning of R--

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:02.270
I mean, I can say
without reservation

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:04.880
that I've learned more
about R from Twitter

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:06.920
than any other single source--

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:10.520
but also, finding a community
and building connections

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:11.120
with folks.

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:16.400
I've met so many folks here who
I have known through Twitter.

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:21.680
And I've only used R for three
years, only really kind of got

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:23.870
more involved with this
community in the last couple

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:25.700
years.

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:28.640
The major benefit, if
you're not already aware,

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:31.340
of the Twitter community and
the R community in general

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:33.320
is that it's
extremely welcoming.

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:36.200
And so I know I felt
coming into it, like,

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:38.470
I'm an anthropologist.

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:40.220
I don't have much
quantitative background.

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:41.600
Am I going to be accepted?

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:45.860
And I've never
not felt accepted.

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:48.110
I think because there's such
a diversity of the things

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:52.950
that people do with R and
a number of other reasons,

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:56.000
including, strongly,
the work of R-Ladies,

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:58.850
have made the R community
extremely welcoming.

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:01.670
And so I think putting yourself
out there in that community,

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:03.530
even when you feel
like, you know, who am I

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:06.980
to try and have anything
to say about the world of R

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:09.540
when there's so many people
doing so many amazing things,

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:13.460
it is such a welcoming community
and it is really possible

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:15.040
to find a place in it.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:16.040
JEN HECHT: That's great.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:18.770
I'm smiling, because I've had so
many conversations with people

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:19.270
here.

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:22.160
And I think almost
everyone feels that way.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:24.410
One of the great things about
coming to the conference

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:27.530
is you get to hear amazing
presentations from people

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:28.620
doing cool things.

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:30.950
But I think the
dark side is people

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:35.030
may be wandering around going,
oh, I'm one that doesn't fit.

00:11:35.030 --> 00:11:37.540
I have one other
prepared question.

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:39.830
And before I ask it, I want
to just let people know,

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:42.950
you can ask questions for
our panelists on Sli.do

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:45.500
I think this is probably
pretty routine for folks now.

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:49.760
We're in track one and
the code is Hexagon.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:51.740
So this is a pretty
general question.

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:55.460
But let's make a
broad assumption

00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:57.590
that a lot of people
in the room are maybe

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:01.520
in the earlier stages of
their data science career.

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:05.150
If you had, like, a few
minutes to spend with them

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:09.470
and give them advice about how
to grow that in the best way

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:12.990
possible, what advice would
you share with people?

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:15.010
SYDEAKA WATSON: So I can start.

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:19.430
So my background, I mentioned
that I actually went to school

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:24.140
for statistics, and actually
went into a biostats career,

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:26.690
and that was before I even
knew what data science was.

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:30.740
So when I learned what it
was, I realized, especially

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:33.950
as I started looking through
some of the different job

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:36.350
opportunities,
that they would ask

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:38.620
for this long list of skills.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:39.950
And it was very intimidating.

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:41.635
And we need a person
who knows this

00:12:41.635 --> 00:12:43.010
and they know this
and know this.

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:47.150
And it was just, how can
I get that expertise so

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:50.690
that I can just get my foot in
the door into this first role?

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:52.310
And so that would
be one thing that I

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:57.470
would advise you to think about
is to just not be overwhelmed.

00:12:57.470 --> 00:13:01.160
Just think about some of
the key principles, I guess,

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:04.820
that I would say are
fundamental to data science.

00:13:04.820 --> 00:13:07.460
And I could list it
out as a bulleted list

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:08.823
if you want to write them down.

00:13:08.823 --> 00:13:11.240
I would say that there are
three key areas of data science

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:13.970
that you'd want to have
some proficiency in.

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:16.770
One would be, there's obviously
the mathematical component.

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:19.190
So there's where your machine
learning, or maybe even

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:21.148
your deep learning, your
clustering algorithms,

00:13:21.148 --> 00:13:23.150
all those kinds of
different algorithms,

00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:26.030
even logistic regression,
or ordinary least squares

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:28.190
regression, basic
data analysis--

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:31.040
those types of areas are
very important to get

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:32.600
some proficiency in.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:33.950
The next would be programming.

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:37.550
So obviously having proficiency
in something like R or Python.

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:41.960
Some type of general use
language, such as one of those,

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:43.610
is ideal.

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:45.680
Not just understanding
specifics of how

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:48.240
to use this package
in that module,

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:54.050
but knowing how to program,
how to be a programmer, how

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:56.570
to actually do the loops
and if-then statements

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:58.970
and actually put together
some piece of code

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:00.253
that accomplishes some task.

00:14:00.253 --> 00:14:01.670
So that would be
the second thing.

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:03.712
And the third, I would
say, would be the big data

00:14:03.712 --> 00:14:04.850
technologies.

00:14:04.850 --> 00:14:08.450
So some of you might be familiar
with SQL or Hadoop, Spark,

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:10.910
Hive, things in that ecosystem.

00:14:10.910 --> 00:14:13.340
So having some familiarity
at least on the surface

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:15.270
level in each of
those three areas,

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:17.690
and then developing those
more and more over time

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:20.150
as you get into
whichever specific role

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:22.460
and you learn the specific
needs of that role,

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:25.240
I think that would be a
good place for you to start.

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:26.370
JEN HECHT: Thank you.

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:27.287
GABI DE QUEIROZ: Yeah.

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:34.050
I agree with the job
descriptions and all that.

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:36.080
But I think if you
are beginner, even,

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:39.830
like, big data, some companies,
they are not there yet

00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:44.030
and they are still on the
Excel spreadsheets, CSV files.

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:47.750
So if big data, it's
like a big thing,

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:50.450
like, you are not
there yet, don't worry,

00:14:50.450 --> 00:14:53.630
because I still think there
are some several companies that

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:58.010
are still doing small data,
working with small data.

00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:02.060
But yeah, the job description
is, like, don't even

00:15:02.060 --> 00:15:04.400
bother to learn all
the technologies,

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:09.830
because there is no way--
like, I have no idea how

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:10.990
people know all that.

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:12.290
That is the Unicorn, right?

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:14.990
So the companies,
they want this Unicorn,

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:18.590
and you as a data scientist, you
don't have to know everything,

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:22.100
because if they are
looking for the Unicorn

00:15:22.100 --> 00:15:24.410
and you are the Unicorn,
you're probably not going

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:25.690
to work for that company.

00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:28.460
You are going to be working for
a different company, either.

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:32.540
So if you are trying to
get into the data science,

00:15:32.540 --> 00:15:35.930
I would say apply for jobs.

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:38.420
You are going to feel
that are you not ready

00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:41.300
and that's a feeling that you
are going to have forever.

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:43.890
You are never ready.

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:46.700
So just apply,
apply, apply, apply,

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:49.940
and try to get a sense of how
the interview processes are,

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:51.980
because they vary so much.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:56.750
Like, some companies are
going to be asking questions

00:15:56.750 --> 00:15:58.190
around probability.

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:02.030
Other companies are going to
ask you questions more general,

00:16:02.030 --> 00:16:04.910
like, just to get
your thinking, how

00:16:04.910 --> 00:16:06.780
you think about the problems.

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:10.760
So I just would say, if you are
looking for a job, just apply.

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:15.500
Don't wait, because you
will never be ready.

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:17.990
DAVID KEYES: So my answer
will be very much tailored

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:21.890
to my work, which is
basically as a consultant.

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:25.710
I think I would offer
two pieces of advice.

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:27.320
One is to try and
find a niche if you

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:30.928
are thinking about trying
to do some consulting.

00:16:30.928 --> 00:16:33.470
One of the major advantages of
R is it's multifaceted, right?

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:36.060
You can do so many
different things with R.

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:39.680
And as an R user, you know
that and you get very excited.

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:45.230
I think I've found, as a
consultant working with folks,

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:46.858
it can be overwhelming
to come in--

00:16:46.858 --> 00:16:49.400
because I do training and then
I also sometimes actually will

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:51.440
do some work for folks.

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:54.170
I'll get really excited and I'll
say, well, we can do this in R

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:58.190
and that in R and this
in these 10 other things,

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:00.000
and that can be
overwhelming for folks.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:01.708
And so I think really
kind of like trying

00:17:01.708 --> 00:17:05.000
to focus on what you can
particularly offer to a client

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.000
if you are thinking about
doing some consulting.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:09.589
So for example, I focus,
when I'm not training,

00:17:09.589 --> 00:17:12.319
I mostly do data visualization,
because that's an area

00:17:12.319 --> 00:17:14.930
that I'm pretty strong in.

00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:18.569
So having that niche,
I think, is important.

00:17:18.569 --> 00:17:24.069
The-- well, I totally blanked
on what my other thing was.

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:25.707
I will come back to
it if I remember.

00:17:25.707 --> 00:17:26.290
JEN HECHT: OK.

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:27.410
Cool.

00:17:27.410 --> 00:17:30.500
I'll just add that in my
experience working in HR,

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:32.390
most job descriptions
are terrible.

00:17:32.390 --> 00:17:35.030
[LAUGHS] Honestly.

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:37.195
Like, especially
in data science,

00:17:37.195 --> 00:17:38.570
half the things
on there, there's

00:17:38.570 --> 00:17:40.410
no human that could
possibly do it.

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:42.410
So you shouldn't let
that get in your head.

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:44.140
I'll just add that.

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:46.430
OK, so with your
permission, panel,

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:49.520
I'm going to go to some
questions from our folks

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:50.910
out here.

00:17:50.910 --> 00:17:55.130
The first one has 30 people
asking this question.

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:57.890
I'm trying to decide whether
to maintain a technical role

00:17:57.890 --> 00:18:00.920
or try to shift into
management and decision-making.

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:04.595
Any tips on what to do
if I want to do both?

00:18:04.595 --> 00:18:05.470
SYDEAKA WATSON: Yeah.

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:08.330
That's a struggle
that I go through.

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:11.260
And before becoming
a manager was--

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:13.210
the thing that I was
most afraid of is,

00:18:13.210 --> 00:18:16.900
like, am I going to be in
this sea of bureaucracy

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:19.870
and then I'll lose all my
technical skills that I worked

00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:23.650
so hard for several years?

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:24.520
I was not sure.

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:26.800
So I talked to a few folks
in the company, especially

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:32.020
the company that I work for,
asking them, how was your path?

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:35.590
And then how do you
feel about keeping up

00:18:35.590 --> 00:18:38.440
with the technical
piece as well?

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:42.940
So in large corporations,
in particular,

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:45.340
you don't have to go
one way or the other.

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:47.740
You can kind of
go in the middle.

00:18:47.740 --> 00:18:50.180
And you can also, like,
always go back and forth.

00:18:50.180 --> 00:18:53.320
So you can become a manager and
go for the management track,

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:56.020
but you can also come back
to the technical track

00:18:56.020 --> 00:18:57.760
for a few years, and
then come back again

00:18:57.760 --> 00:19:00.680
to the management track.

00:19:00.680 --> 00:19:02.440
So that's the beauty
of some companies

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:08.200
where you can go back and
forth, so you keep developing.

00:19:08.200 --> 00:19:14.590
The other thing is try
to get some time for you.

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:18.730
Like, let's say on Fridays, I
try to schedule no meetings.

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:22.540
And then I go to our corner
in the room in the office,

00:19:22.540 --> 00:19:25.270
and then I put my headphone
on, and then I just

00:19:25.270 --> 00:19:27.310
do some coding or code review.

00:19:27.310 --> 00:19:29.740
So that's my strategy
to keep me happy,

00:19:29.740 --> 00:19:32.980
because there is a piece of
me that I miss if I'm not

00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:34.763
doing technical work.

00:19:34.763 --> 00:19:35.430
JEN HECHT: Yeah.

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:37.750
Makes sense.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:40.090
SYDEAKA WATSON: I've had
that same fear, actually.

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:41.923
So I was actually kind
of interested to hear

00:19:41.923 --> 00:19:43.810
what other people had
to say about this,

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:46.690
because I've been invited
to go into management

00:19:46.690 --> 00:19:50.380
and into director-level
roles, but I like

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:51.880
having my hands on the data.

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:54.100
I like having my
finger on the pulse

00:19:54.100 --> 00:19:57.850
of all the different types
of cool tools and strategies

00:19:57.850 --> 00:19:58.700
that are out there.

00:19:58.700 --> 00:20:00.350
And so I don't
want to lose that.

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:03.130
So part of me is nervous.

00:20:03.130 --> 00:20:04.990
So I haven't made that jump.

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:08.620
But I would say that
was something that--

00:20:08.620 --> 00:20:11.110
I really like those tips--

00:20:11.110 --> 00:20:15.040
doing things that help you to
continue to work those muscles.

00:20:15.040 --> 00:20:16.960
So I know some
people who actively

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:19.570
had to go on
HackerRank every day

00:20:19.570 --> 00:20:22.300
and do some coding
exercises in the morning

00:20:22.300 --> 00:20:26.180
or actually participate
in code reviews and so on.

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:29.150
So I would imagine that would
be a really good strategy.

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:30.070
Yeah.

00:20:30.070 --> 00:20:32.470
GABI DE QUEIROZ: Just one
thing that I was thinking now,

00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:34.900
so if there is anything
that I need to measure--

00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:38.190
let's say I need to measure
something on my team

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:40.780
and then I'm like, yes,
I have some time for me

00:20:40.780 --> 00:20:44.750
to create our shiny where I'm
going to measure something.

00:20:44.750 --> 00:20:48.520
So I try to translate
tasks that I

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:55.150
need to do in a coding exercise,
so I keep up with my coding.

00:20:55.150 --> 00:20:56.590
Yeah.

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:57.250
We can talk.

00:20:57.250 --> 00:20:59.028
Like, it's a big jump.

00:20:59.028 --> 00:21:00.070
It's a big jump for sure.

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:01.600
It's so different.

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:04.450
But also, you are
developing other pieces

00:21:04.450 --> 00:21:07.930
of your brain, your
skills, that is

00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:11.650
going to help you also in the
future as a coder, programmer.

00:21:11.650 --> 00:21:14.650
So another example
that I was thinking

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:18.280
is one day I had
to write something.

00:21:18.280 --> 00:21:19.870
And then it came.

00:21:19.870 --> 00:21:21.880
Like, things were
coming through my mind.

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:24.580
And I'm like, OK, I didn't
forget those things.

00:21:24.580 --> 00:21:29.707
And then I became
a better searcher,

00:21:29.707 --> 00:21:31.040
or I don't know how to say that.

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:33.250
But I can now
search even better,

00:21:33.250 --> 00:21:35.590
because there is some
piece of my brain

00:21:35.590 --> 00:21:37.220
that got developed
a little bit better,

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:40.600
so I know exactly how to look
for answers, where before I

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:42.460
would struggle much more.

00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:43.600
So I don't know.

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:46.030
Yeah, it's something
interesting, I think.

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:47.770
And if you have
the ability to, you

00:21:47.770 --> 00:21:49.720
know, I want to
become a manager,

00:21:49.720 --> 00:21:52.100
I'm going to give,
like, a time--

00:21:52.100 --> 00:21:54.160
I'm going to timebox
this for two years.

00:21:54.160 --> 00:21:55.900
I'm going to try it
out for two years.

00:21:55.900 --> 00:21:58.730
It's like, if I don't
like it, can I go back?

00:21:58.730 --> 00:21:59.948
JEN HECHT: Yeah.

00:21:59.948 --> 00:22:00.490
That's great.

00:22:00.490 --> 00:22:02.470
And David, from what
you were saying before,

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:06.070
you have your hands in doing
direct work as a consultant

00:22:06.070 --> 00:22:08.070
and data visualization,
and you're teaching.

00:22:08.070 --> 00:22:10.357
So you've been able to
combine those a bit as well.

00:22:10.357 --> 00:22:12.940
DAVID KEYES: And if you work for
yourself, you get to do both.

00:22:12.940 --> 00:22:14.172
JEN HECHT: Yeah.

00:22:14.172 --> 00:22:15.380
DAVID KEYES: Better or worse.

00:22:15.380 --> 00:22:17.540
JEN HECHT: Yeah, that's great.

00:22:17.540 --> 00:22:20.530
OK, another very
popular question up here

00:22:20.530 --> 00:22:23.800
is, can you talk about
salary negotiations?

00:22:23.800 --> 00:22:24.310
Any tips?

00:22:26.508 --> 00:22:27.800
SYDEAKA WATSON: OK, I'll start.

00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:36.880
[LAUGHS] All right, so I'll
start with a story, something

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:38.280
that actually happened.

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:41.250
So I was a contractor
working at a company,

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:43.500
along with a friend, a male
colleague, who was also

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:45.250
a contractor.

00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:48.870
And he ended up deciding
to go into another company.

00:22:48.870 --> 00:22:53.070
And as they were
negotiating his salary,

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:56.280
they asked him, how much
are you currently making,

00:22:56.280 --> 00:23:00.168
which, of course, is the trap,
because the next company wants

00:23:00.168 --> 00:23:02.460
to just kind of keep you
around where you are and maybe

00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:04.500
give you a little bit more.

00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:06.750
And that is the
trap question that

00:23:06.750 --> 00:23:13.950
keeps the income inequality for
men versus women so prevalent.

00:23:13.950 --> 00:23:15.390
But he got that question.

00:23:15.390 --> 00:23:20.680
And so he gave a very
creatively high answer, which

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:21.930
I don't necessarily recommend.

00:23:21.930 --> 00:23:24.060
But I think he said
something like, I'm currently

00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:25.410
making $200,000.

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:26.660
And he wasn't making $200,000.

00:23:26.660 --> 00:23:29.260
He said, I'm currently
making $200,000.

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:32.670
And so the response was, oh,
there's no way in the world

00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:34.620
I can pay you $200,000.

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:36.660
The best I could do is 175.

00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:38.910
Is that OK?

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:41.430
Which, to me, I would
have never even dreamed

00:23:41.430 --> 00:23:44.340
of giving some sort of
number that high, because I

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:45.960
think in a lot of
times, we don't

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:47.790
value ourselves to be that.

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:49.878
I'm not worth $175,000.

00:23:49.878 --> 00:23:51.420
You might be saying
that to yourself.

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:53.850
Well, maybe I'll give
them a lower number,

00:23:53.850 --> 00:23:56.700
or I don't want them to think
that I'm just too greedy,

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:58.570
or something like that.

00:23:58.570 --> 00:24:02.340
So I think what helps in
those types of situations

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:06.540
would be to, first of all,
understand the market,

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:09.540
understand what a
reasonable amount of money

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:12.680
would be for that particular
role for your area,

00:24:12.680 --> 00:24:16.680
like, a geographic area,
and also for your specialty

00:24:16.680 --> 00:24:19.690
and for your years
of experience.

00:24:19.690 --> 00:24:23.340
And the worst that they
could do is say no, right?

00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:25.530
I mean, you give them a
reasonable number that

00:24:25.530 --> 00:24:28.920
is in line with a salary
that you can justify,

00:24:28.920 --> 00:24:31.650
the worst they could say is,
no, it's a little bit lower.

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:33.960
But I definitely just
encourage you to, like I said,

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:39.450
just think a little bit more
outside of the box and value

00:24:39.450 --> 00:24:41.857
yourselves, I guess, maybe
higher than potential--

00:24:41.857 --> 00:24:44.190
because they obviously have
a vested interest in keeping

00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:45.750
your salary as low as possible.

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:48.940
They just want you in the door
in the cheapest way possible.

00:24:48.940 --> 00:24:52.500
So yeah, I highly encourage
you to actually negotiate

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:54.370
your salary.

00:24:54.370 --> 00:24:56.788
JEN HECHT: You raise a great
point about the prior salary,

00:24:56.788 --> 00:24:58.080
because that's totally illegal.

00:24:58.080 --> 00:25:00.330
I know it is in
Massachusetts now to ask

00:25:00.330 --> 00:25:02.160
that question in an interview.

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:04.500
I don't know how many other
states have gone there.

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:06.930
But that's the reason why
is that it automatically

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:09.420
kind of drags people
down, especially those who

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:14.330
are unwilling to get
creative in their ask.

00:25:14.330 --> 00:25:17.130
GABI DE QUEIROZ: One thing
that I kind of learned to do

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:20.100
is to have a network
of people that I

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:23.940
have some kind of like open
space or open conversation,

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:26.310
where I can ask the questions.

00:25:26.310 --> 00:25:29.175
I can say, I'm
looking for a job.

00:25:29.175 --> 00:25:31.980
I have this amount of
years of experience.

00:25:31.980 --> 00:25:35.370
And how much do you
think I should ask for?

00:25:35.370 --> 00:25:37.860
And they say, oh,
I'm a senior person.

00:25:37.860 --> 00:25:40.860
I know that my company
is paying around this.

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:45.180
So I kind of like did a survey
with my friends or the people

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:49.700
that I know and asked them, what
is the average or the interval

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:53.320
that you think I
should be asking for?

00:25:53.320 --> 00:25:55.530
The other good thing
and tip is if you

00:25:55.530 --> 00:26:00.000
have any friend that is manager
or hiring manager, ask them,

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:04.035
because they know a lot about
the salaries in the industry.

00:26:04.035 --> 00:26:05.370
JEN HECHT: Yeah.

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:07.350
And the other thing I
would say about that,

00:26:07.350 --> 00:26:10.500
too, is-- and you want to
be careful with this advice,

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:13.920
because if you're working in
a very conservative industry,

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:16.170
this might be harder to do.

00:26:16.170 --> 00:26:18.900
But you should be able to
talk with your colleagues

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:22.890
in your current job
about what you're making.

00:26:22.890 --> 00:26:25.230
And that can be
very scary, but it's

00:26:25.230 --> 00:26:26.520
a good way to get information.

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:29.910
And I think the workplace
is changing a lot.

00:26:29.910 --> 00:26:33.000
One of the positive things is,
I think, more transparency.

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:34.500
But if you have
people that you work

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:37.610
with in your same
discipline that you trust,

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:40.980
I'd encourage you to do that
even where you are right now.

00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:43.890
And again, I've worked
in a range of places.

00:26:43.890 --> 00:26:47.040
I think most places are kind
of stopping this practice.

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:49.690
It used to be like, oh,
you cannot discuss salary,

00:26:49.690 --> 00:26:53.160
which I think also keeps a lot
of people in the dark about

00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:54.280
what the real ranges are.

00:26:57.060 --> 00:26:59.570
OK, I'm going to go
to the next question.

00:26:59.570 --> 00:27:01.480
How do you decide which
technical skills are

00:27:01.480 --> 00:27:04.240
worth the time investment to
really learn versus having

00:27:04.240 --> 00:27:05.500
a passing acquaintance with?

00:27:08.950 --> 00:27:14.890
SYDEAKA WATSON: I think it would
depend on your specific role

00:27:14.890 --> 00:27:18.400
and whether or not
that is something

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:22.670
that is needed for your
job or for your growth.

00:27:22.670 --> 00:27:26.890
So I think about, for example,
going back to that list of--

00:27:26.890 --> 00:27:30.010
that massively long list of
different types of machine

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:33.610
learning tools and
software and models

00:27:33.610 --> 00:27:35.590
and all these different
kinds of things.

00:27:35.590 --> 00:27:38.950
In your particular job, maybe
you only need clustering.

00:27:38.950 --> 00:27:41.200
Like, they need somebody
who's an expert at clustering

00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:43.060
and they need somebody
who understands

00:27:43.060 --> 00:27:46.870
all the different ins and
outs of a very small number

00:27:46.870 --> 00:27:48.580
of models.

00:27:48.580 --> 00:27:51.910
In that sense, maybe
it's worth the effort

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:55.120
of really digging in
and understanding those.

00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:56.780
But the other side
of that, obviously,

00:27:56.780 --> 00:28:01.780
is that what that
does is that keeps you

00:28:01.780 --> 00:28:05.590
in the expert role for where you
are now or maybe with the team

00:28:05.590 --> 00:28:07.000
that you're currently in.

00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:11.470
So another view of that will be
to say, where do I want to go,

00:28:11.470 --> 00:28:12.970
or what is the
type of job that I

00:28:12.970 --> 00:28:14.500
want to have or
the type of career

00:28:14.500 --> 00:28:17.050
that I want to have
for myself eventually?

00:28:17.050 --> 00:28:18.880
When I answer that
question for myself,

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:23.173
I want to be not just the best
data scientist at my company,

00:28:23.173 --> 00:28:25.090
I want to be the best
data scientist possible,

00:28:25.090 --> 00:28:28.270
so that any company can look at
me and all the different types

00:28:28.270 --> 00:28:30.200
of experiences that
I've had and say,

00:28:30.200 --> 00:28:32.200
wow, she really
understands a lot

00:28:32.200 --> 00:28:34.360
of different kinds of
tools, she understands

00:28:34.360 --> 00:28:38.130
that AI, for example, that's
the buzzword of the day,

00:28:38.130 --> 00:28:40.420
and a lot of companies
are interested in that,

00:28:40.420 --> 00:28:44.200
and so she's the person
that I would want to call,

00:28:44.200 --> 00:28:46.430
because she has that
type of expertise.

00:28:46.430 --> 00:28:48.340
So I would definitely
challenge you

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:50.800
to think about your
career and the path

00:28:50.800 --> 00:28:53.290
that you want to go on, and
figure out whether or not

00:28:53.290 --> 00:28:55.540
some of these different types
of tools will be useful.

00:28:55.540 --> 00:28:57.890
And some of that requires
reading in the literature,

00:28:57.890 --> 00:28:59.920
but also looking at
different use cases

00:28:59.920 --> 00:29:02.170
to see how other people
are using it and the value

00:29:02.170 --> 00:29:04.628
that they're able to get from
some of these different types

00:29:04.628 --> 00:29:05.650
of tools or models.

00:29:08.622 --> 00:29:10.330
JEN HECHT: This question
actually came up

00:29:10.330 --> 00:29:12.910
in a session I was in earlier.

00:29:12.910 --> 00:29:15.400
What is the difference between
a data analyst and data

00:29:15.400 --> 00:29:16.690
scientist?

00:29:16.690 --> 00:29:19.370
In your experience,
is there a difference?

00:29:19.370 --> 00:29:21.910
GABI DE QUEIROZ: So
in some companies,

00:29:21.910 --> 00:29:23.830
there is no distinction.

00:29:23.830 --> 00:29:25.180
It's more like a title.

00:29:25.180 --> 00:29:29.780
But from my experience,
data analyst

00:29:29.780 --> 00:29:32.960
is doing more querying
the data, getting the data

00:29:32.960 --> 00:29:34.940
from a database,
let's say using SQL,

00:29:34.940 --> 00:29:40.100
creating reports, where data
scientists go one step further,

00:29:40.100 --> 00:29:42.860
where they also do
the data cleaning,

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:46.160
munging, and querying the
data, but also is involved

00:29:46.160 --> 00:29:48.500
on the modeling itself.

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:50.210
So that's my experience.

00:29:50.210 --> 00:29:53.210
But I think it's kind
of like a gray area.

00:29:53.210 --> 00:29:55.370
Like, some companies,
they call data analyst

00:29:55.370 --> 00:29:58.960
what I consider
being data scientist.

00:29:58.960 --> 00:30:02.870
And they have different names,
yeah, even more names than data

00:30:02.870 --> 00:30:04.340
analyst and data scientist.

00:30:04.340 --> 00:30:06.800
JEN HECHT: Yeah.

00:30:06.800 --> 00:30:10.310
SYDEAKA WATSON: These
are all squishy terms.

00:30:10.310 --> 00:30:13.520
So I guess maybe just
thinking about some

00:30:13.520 --> 00:30:15.590
of the different
roles I've had, people

00:30:15.590 --> 00:30:21.530
who've had the data
scientist role, they tend

00:30:21.530 --> 00:30:23.060
to have a little
bit more training,

00:30:23.060 --> 00:30:25.290
maybe a little bit
more education,

00:30:25.290 --> 00:30:30.290
like, formal training in maybe
like a math or statistics

00:30:30.290 --> 00:30:34.040
or physics or
something like that.

00:30:34.040 --> 00:30:35.030
They tend to, again.

00:30:35.030 --> 00:30:37.580
Again, there's no
hard, fast rule

00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:39.980
for any of these
different types of things.

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:42.860
And they tend to have a
little bit more expertise

00:30:42.860 --> 00:30:46.610
in some of the more advanced
types of modeling scenarios,

00:30:46.610 --> 00:30:49.280
whereas maybe a
data analyst would

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:51.410
be very proficient
in some of the data

00:30:51.410 --> 00:30:57.770
engineering tasks, or pulling
different types of SQL queries,

00:30:57.770 --> 00:31:01.680
and maybe creating some
dashboards or some reports.

00:31:01.680 --> 00:31:06.410
But the deeper insights
of trying to understand,

00:31:06.410 --> 00:31:11.420
maybe starting with
the question, of why

00:31:11.420 --> 00:31:15.860
are we losing so much money
in this particular area

00:31:15.860 --> 00:31:19.945
of our company and this kind
of product in our company?

00:31:19.945 --> 00:31:21.320
When I think of
a data scientist,

00:31:21.320 --> 00:31:24.020
I think of a scientist,
where you actually have

00:31:24.020 --> 00:31:25.730
to start from the beginning.

00:31:25.730 --> 00:31:28.790
Nobody's handing you a CSV
or telling you specifically

00:31:28.790 --> 00:31:31.400
which types of data to look
at or what types of models

00:31:31.400 --> 00:31:32.600
to use and so on.

00:31:32.600 --> 00:31:34.370
You're starting from
scratch, and you're

00:31:34.370 --> 00:31:37.100
able to go through that
entire scientific process

00:31:37.100 --> 00:31:40.730
and figure out where
your hypotheses are,

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:43.550
testing those, maybe validating
those, maybe circling

00:31:43.550 --> 00:31:46.130
back and going back
to the beginning.

00:31:46.130 --> 00:31:48.620
And so that entire process,
I would think of that

00:31:48.620 --> 00:31:51.650
as that the person that has that
type of holistic set of skills

00:31:51.650 --> 00:31:54.050
is a data scientist as well.

00:31:54.050 --> 00:31:57.270
GABI DE QUEIROZ: And one last
thing is data scientists,

00:31:57.270 --> 00:31:59.000
they usually make
much more money--

00:31:59.000 --> 00:31:59.430
SYDEAKA WATSON: Yes.

00:31:59.430 --> 00:32:00.290
GABI DE QUEIROZ:
--than data analysts.

00:32:00.290 --> 00:32:00.720
SYDEAKA WATSON: There is that.

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:02.510
GABI DE QUEIROZ: So
if you are in doubt

00:32:02.510 --> 00:32:04.390
if you should write
in your resume

00:32:04.390 --> 00:32:06.620
that you are data analyst
or data scientist,

00:32:06.620 --> 00:32:07.952
go for data scientist.

00:32:07.952 --> 00:32:09.160
SYDEAKA WATSON: That's right.

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:09.880
JEN HECHT: Yeah.

00:32:09.880 --> 00:32:12.350
Well, it gets complicated,
because in some companies,

00:32:12.350 --> 00:32:16.280
there's the official job
title that's owned by HR,

00:32:16.280 --> 00:32:19.203
and then they'll let people
make up business titles that

00:32:19.203 --> 00:32:20.120
say anything you want.

00:32:20.120 --> 00:32:23.323
So I've seen cases of somebody
gets hired as a business

00:32:23.323 --> 00:32:25.490
intelligence analyst, and
three weeks later, they're

00:32:25.490 --> 00:32:26.300
a data scientist.

00:32:26.300 --> 00:32:29.730
You're like, wow, that was fast.

00:32:29.730 --> 00:32:30.290
OK.

00:32:30.290 --> 00:32:34.730
So a good question
here about how

00:32:34.730 --> 00:32:37.580
do you handle being in a job
with little or no mentorship

00:32:37.580 --> 00:32:38.570
opportunities?

00:32:42.252 --> 00:32:44.335
SYDEAKA WATSON: I kind of
touched on this earlier.

00:32:47.210 --> 00:32:48.530
I've definitely been that--

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:51.900
how can I say it?

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:55.097
I've been that person that
has the set of skills,

00:32:55.097 --> 00:32:57.180
that one particular set
of skills that nobody else

00:32:57.180 --> 00:32:58.530
in the company has.

00:32:58.530 --> 00:33:02.690
So they literally cannot
speak to me about it.

00:33:02.690 --> 00:33:04.200
And not to say
that I'm inherently

00:33:04.200 --> 00:33:05.880
smarter than them necessarily.

00:33:05.880 --> 00:33:07.500
Everybody has their
own specialty.

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:08.970
But maybe I'm the
only one that has

00:33:08.970 --> 00:33:10.300
studied this particular area.

00:33:10.300 --> 00:33:15.760
And so I can't talk to them
about those kinds of topics.

00:33:15.760 --> 00:33:19.950
And so what I have to do
is create my own network.

00:33:19.950 --> 00:33:23.370
Just in the course of
being a person who's

00:33:23.370 --> 00:33:26.648
interesting in developing my
network in Dallas, for example,

00:33:26.648 --> 00:33:27.690
I go to different events.

00:33:27.690 --> 00:33:30.522
I go to different talks
and just talk to people

00:33:30.522 --> 00:33:32.730
and find out what they're
interested in, so then when

00:33:32.730 --> 00:33:34.650
I come across some
kind of problem

00:33:34.650 --> 00:33:36.630
and I just like, oh, I
don't know what to do

00:33:36.630 --> 00:33:39.890
or where should I go next
with this, oh, wait a minute,

00:33:39.890 --> 00:33:42.720
David knows this.

00:33:42.720 --> 00:33:44.190
He sounds like
the kind of person

00:33:44.190 --> 00:33:45.815
that would have that
kind of expertise.

00:33:45.815 --> 00:33:48.030
Maybe I can tap him.

00:33:48.030 --> 00:33:49.530
So that's one area
when you think

00:33:49.530 --> 00:33:51.300
about technical mentorship.

00:33:51.300 --> 00:33:55.320
But the other path would be
just growth for your career.

00:33:55.320 --> 00:33:58.598
So just figuring out, I want
to be a director someday

00:33:58.598 --> 00:34:00.390
or I want to be a
technical leader someday,

00:34:00.390 --> 00:34:02.490
how can I do that?

00:34:02.490 --> 00:34:05.790
And so some of that
is just finding

00:34:05.790 --> 00:34:08.250
people who have gone
through that path

00:34:08.250 --> 00:34:10.190
that you're trying
to go through.

00:34:10.190 --> 00:34:11.940
And that could be
within your own company.

00:34:11.940 --> 00:34:15.630
That could be for people who
work in similar types of roles

00:34:15.630 --> 00:34:17.790
as you're trying to
get into, maybe who've

00:34:17.790 --> 00:34:19.830
been in different companies.

00:34:19.830 --> 00:34:24.030
So try to just be creative about
finding people that can inspire

00:34:24.030 --> 00:34:26.040
you in the ways that you need.

00:34:26.040 --> 00:34:27.239
JEN HECHT: That's great.

00:34:27.239 --> 00:34:30.600
David, I'm going to
direct this one to you.

00:34:30.600 --> 00:34:32.730
Can you recommend any
good Twitter accounts

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:34.380
to follow to learn
more about data?

00:34:34.380 --> 00:34:37.260
And already mentioned you
highly leverage Twitter.

00:34:37.260 --> 00:34:39.850
What would you recommend?

00:34:39.850 --> 00:34:42.659
DAVID KEYES: Well, if you're not
already following Mara Averick,

00:34:42.659 --> 00:34:45.528
that's probably the best
place to start, definitely

00:34:45.528 --> 00:34:47.445
where I think I've learned
more than anything.

00:34:50.380 --> 00:34:51.780
Jeez.

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:53.954
There's so many people.

00:34:53.954 --> 00:34:56.400
I'm trying to think of
some other ones that

00:34:56.400 --> 00:34:58.650
have been particularly
informative for me.

00:34:58.650 --> 00:35:00.740
I would say, this isn't
an account, but the--

00:35:00.740 --> 00:35:02.520
and particularly when
you're developing

00:35:02.520 --> 00:35:04.110
in data visualization
especially,

00:35:04.110 --> 00:35:06.810
the #TidyTuesday hashtag
has been really helpful

00:35:06.810 --> 00:35:10.230
for me to see folks who
are doing some really

00:35:10.230 --> 00:35:12.690
interesting data visualization.

00:35:12.690 --> 00:35:14.692
That's been a helpful one.

00:35:14.692 --> 00:35:16.582
Yeah, I think I'd
probably start there.

00:35:16.582 --> 00:35:17.680
JEN HECHT: Great.

00:35:17.680 --> 00:35:18.930
Anyone else have any thoughts?

00:35:18.930 --> 00:35:21.660
GABI DE QUEIROZ: What
about the #rstats hashtag?

00:35:21.660 --> 00:35:22.410
DAVID KEYES: Yeah.

00:35:22.410 --> 00:35:22.910
Sorry.

00:35:22.910 --> 00:35:23.945
I assumed that was--

00:35:23.945 --> 00:35:25.130
[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]

00:35:25.130 --> 00:35:27.330
But yes, #rstats hashtag
if you're not already

00:35:27.330 --> 00:35:28.870
following that as well.

00:35:28.870 --> 00:35:31.780
The other thing, actually,
looking over at you

00:35:31.780 --> 00:35:33.780
for a second, Gabi, makes
me think that I really

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:39.930
like the R-Ladies, @WeAreRLadies
rotating curator, because it's

00:35:39.930 --> 00:35:42.090
really fun to see
kind of each week.

00:35:42.090 --> 00:35:44.915
It's a different person
who curates the account.

00:35:44.915 --> 00:35:47.040
And it's really fun just
to see the different types

00:35:47.040 --> 00:35:49.380
of work that people are doing
and the different packages they

00:35:49.380 --> 00:35:49.880
use.

00:35:49.880 --> 00:35:51.550
So I really like
that account as well.

00:35:51.550 --> 00:35:52.258
JEN HECHT: Great.

00:35:54.330 --> 00:35:58.450
So this question is more
about R versus other things.

00:35:58.450 --> 00:36:01.980
So I'm fundamentally a
generalist in my overall skills

00:36:01.980 --> 00:36:03.990
and my R skills,
breadth not depth.

00:36:03.990 --> 00:36:06.810
Is that a viable way to build
a career or is specializing

00:36:06.810 --> 00:36:08.448
essential?

00:36:08.448 --> 00:36:09.490
SYDEAKA WATSON: For sure.

00:36:12.668 --> 00:36:14.210
JEN HECHT: It's
valid, you're saying?

00:36:14.210 --> 00:36:14.810
Yeah.

00:36:14.810 --> 00:36:16.393
GABI DE QUEIROZ: No,
like, this person

00:36:16.393 --> 00:36:18.045
is saying that all
they know, it's R?

00:36:18.045 --> 00:36:19.420
JEN HECHT: They're
saying they're

00:36:19.420 --> 00:36:23.260
a generalist in their overall
skills and their R skills.

00:36:23.260 --> 00:36:26.860
And they're wondering, is that
a viable way to build a career

00:36:26.860 --> 00:36:30.400
or should they be specializing
within that, either in data

00:36:30.400 --> 00:36:32.047
science or in R, I guess?

00:36:32.047 --> 00:36:33.880
GABI DE QUEIROZ: Well,
I think, again, there

00:36:33.880 --> 00:36:36.970
is a broad of, like, jobs.

00:36:36.970 --> 00:36:41.840
So you can be a generalist or
you can be a very specific.

00:36:41.840 --> 00:36:43.400
It all depends.

00:36:43.400 --> 00:36:44.650
I think there is--

00:36:44.650 --> 00:36:46.490
maybe you have a
better opinion than I.

00:36:46.490 --> 00:36:48.340
But I think there is
space for everybody

00:36:48.340 --> 00:36:53.440
for being a generalist and to
be a very, like, domain expert.

00:36:53.440 --> 00:36:54.502
SYDEAKA WATSON: Right.

00:36:54.502 --> 00:36:56.960
And I think I touched on this
a little bit in the beginning

00:36:56.960 --> 00:37:00.070
when I was thinking about
the different types of ways

00:37:00.070 --> 00:37:05.200
to classify the different types
of skills that are required

00:37:05.200 --> 00:37:06.760
as part of a data scientist.

00:37:06.760 --> 00:37:08.770
And so I was saying,
if I wanted to just

00:37:08.770 --> 00:37:15.880
be a person that a general
data science company would find

00:37:15.880 --> 00:37:18.940
attractive, then I
would have proficiency

00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:22.360
in machine learning,
programming, and big data

00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:23.560
technologies.

00:37:23.560 --> 00:37:25.878
And as our esteemed
panelist said earlier,

00:37:25.878 --> 00:37:28.420
some of those companies might
not necessarily care about some

00:37:28.420 --> 00:37:29.140
of those, right?

00:37:29.140 --> 00:37:30.760
So they might not care
about the big data part.

00:37:30.760 --> 00:37:32.680
Maybe they only care
about the other two.

00:37:32.680 --> 00:37:36.880
But when I think about it,
again, unless you've already

00:37:36.880 --> 00:37:41.260
decided specifically, I know
that this job in particular

00:37:41.260 --> 00:37:44.290
is what I want, and I
know the specific set

00:37:44.290 --> 00:37:46.000
of skills that are
required for that job,

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:47.500
and I'm just going
to keep on honing

00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:51.610
those skills, unless you're
doing that, I would challenge

00:37:51.610 --> 00:37:54.760
you to branch out
a little bit more,

00:37:54.760 --> 00:37:56.330
learn about other
types of things.

00:37:56.330 --> 00:37:58.540
But obviously, you don't
want to get to the point

00:37:58.540 --> 00:38:01.920
where you're just picking
up every single kind of tool

00:38:01.920 --> 00:38:04.647
or every type of
model or expertise.

00:38:04.647 --> 00:38:06.730
At some point, you're going
to have to specialize,

00:38:06.730 --> 00:38:08.730
because the people who
have very specific domain

00:38:08.730 --> 00:38:11.530
experience, like baseball,
like telecommunications,

00:38:11.530 --> 00:38:14.380
and so on, they really
understand the data.

00:38:14.380 --> 00:38:17.140
So they can go
beyond just saying,

00:38:17.140 --> 00:38:18.642
I understand that
this column seems

00:38:18.642 --> 00:38:19.850
to be related to this column.

00:38:19.850 --> 00:38:21.850
They really understand
all the different ways

00:38:21.850 --> 00:38:26.567
that extra knowledge
can inform how well you

00:38:26.567 --> 00:38:28.900
can get some insights from
some of those different types

00:38:28.900 --> 00:38:29.570
of projects.

00:38:29.570 --> 00:38:33.247
So I would encourage you
for your specific area,

00:38:33.247 --> 00:38:35.080
once you've actually
determined that there's

00:38:35.080 --> 00:38:37.510
a specific area that is of
interest to you, that you

00:38:37.510 --> 00:38:40.480
learn, you take the time to
develop that domain experience.

00:38:40.480 --> 00:38:44.260
And the good thing about it
is that if you get into it

00:38:44.260 --> 00:38:47.120
and you realize, eh, I kind
of thought I would like this,

00:38:47.120 --> 00:38:49.630
but I don't like it so much,
then you can get experience

00:38:49.630 --> 00:38:50.680
in something else.

00:38:50.680 --> 00:38:53.920
So yeah, just try
different things.

00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:55.420
But I definitely
would encourage you

00:38:55.420 --> 00:39:00.178
to have some type of
specialization in some areas.

00:39:00.178 --> 00:39:01.490
JEN HECHT: Great.

00:39:01.490 --> 00:39:04.070
We have a couple of questions
sort of in this topic area

00:39:04.070 --> 00:39:06.680
about how you have
dealt with being

00:39:06.680 --> 00:39:09.470
perhaps the only or first
woman or person of color

00:39:09.470 --> 00:39:11.305
in your organization or team.

00:39:11.305 --> 00:39:12.680
Do you have any
advice for people

00:39:12.680 --> 00:39:14.750
who might be, in
addition to trying

00:39:14.750 --> 00:39:16.430
to get into data
science, also dealing

00:39:16.430 --> 00:39:20.830
with issues in that area?

00:39:20.830 --> 00:39:24.520
SYDEAKA WATSON: So I have been
in that scenario from the woman

00:39:24.520 --> 00:39:26.290
and also the person
of color scenario

00:39:26.290 --> 00:39:29.330
in so many of the roles
that I've been in--

00:39:29.330 --> 00:39:32.730
actually, I want to say
every role that I've been in.

00:39:32.730 --> 00:39:34.180
And yeah.

00:39:34.180 --> 00:39:39.340
And so one of the things
that happens, commonly,

00:39:39.340 --> 00:39:42.160
is that as the one
person, you feel like you

00:39:42.160 --> 00:39:44.710
have to be the representative.

00:39:44.710 --> 00:39:47.920
If I mess up, then now
all black people mess up,

00:39:47.920 --> 00:39:50.590
right, you know, that's just how
we are, or all women mess up,

00:39:50.590 --> 00:39:51.250
right?

00:39:51.250 --> 00:39:53.350
So some things you just kind
of have to forgive yourself

00:39:53.350 --> 00:39:55.450
and realize that you're human
just like everybody else.

00:39:55.450 --> 00:39:57.242
You make mistakes just
like everybody else.

00:39:57.242 --> 00:40:01.300
And just take those as growing
opportunities to get better.

00:40:01.300 --> 00:40:04.120
But some of the challenges
are external, some of them

00:40:04.120 --> 00:40:04.900
are internal.

00:40:04.900 --> 00:40:08.470
So I definitely would just
encourage you to realize, yeah,

00:40:08.470 --> 00:40:09.910
I'm a good person, too.

00:40:09.910 --> 00:40:10.840
I'm very smart.

00:40:10.840 --> 00:40:14.000
And I have a lot of great
ideas and good things to offer.

00:40:14.000 --> 00:40:16.570
And so just kind of
keep that in mind

00:40:16.570 --> 00:40:19.496
as you're growing your career.

00:40:19.496 --> 00:40:22.510
GABI DE QUEIROZ: Yeah, also,
don't feel like you have to be

00:40:22.510 --> 00:40:26.710
the one responsible-- like,
let's say there is no diversity

00:40:26.710 --> 00:40:29.580
efforts and then you are going
to be the one doing the whole

00:40:29.580 --> 00:40:31.540
work, because--

00:40:31.540 --> 00:40:34.410
so, like, try to get other
people to help you as well,

00:40:34.410 --> 00:40:35.800
to have allies.

00:40:35.800 --> 00:40:39.750
And also, the outside network is
very important in those times,

00:40:39.750 --> 00:40:44.530
so you don't feel lonely, so
we have this support around you

00:40:44.530 --> 00:40:46.408
that keeps you going.

00:40:46.408 --> 00:40:47.116
JEN HECHT: Great.

00:40:49.750 --> 00:40:53.270
So all of you had
such different paths.

00:40:53.270 --> 00:40:55.120
But we have a
question here about,

00:40:55.120 --> 00:40:57.520
for a young data scientist
trying to get their first job,

00:40:57.520 --> 00:41:00.070
should they be looking at
large companies or startups?

00:41:00.070 --> 00:41:03.760
Do you have any specific
advice about what context might

00:41:03.760 --> 00:41:05.330
be the best place to start?

00:41:11.420 --> 00:41:13.690
GABI DE QUEIROZ: So I
always worked for startups

00:41:13.690 --> 00:41:18.270
until moving to IBM
almost two years ago.

00:41:18.270 --> 00:41:21.620
So I went from a company
where we were 20 people

00:41:21.620 --> 00:41:25.580
to, like, 300,000.

00:41:25.580 --> 00:41:27.020
It's a big jump.

00:41:27.020 --> 00:41:29.090
I don't know what is better.

00:41:29.090 --> 00:41:32.090
I think if you are a
beginner, the best company

00:41:32.090 --> 00:41:34.220
is the company
where, again, you are

00:41:34.220 --> 00:41:37.640
going to have support
mentorship, because it's

00:41:37.640 --> 00:41:41.930
so hard to break
into this career

00:41:41.930 --> 00:41:44.390
without having
someone mentoring you.

00:41:44.390 --> 00:41:47.180
So if there is a startup,
that you have a good network

00:41:47.180 --> 00:41:49.920
and support, yeah, go for it.

00:41:49.920 --> 00:41:53.300
But if you have opportunity
to work for a big company,

00:41:53.300 --> 00:41:54.812
you probably have
more mentorship.

00:41:54.812 --> 00:41:56.270
But also, like,
you are going to be

00:41:56.270 --> 00:41:59.630
more specialized
in a startup, where

00:41:59.630 --> 00:42:03.860
you have to do everything
from getting the data,

00:42:03.860 --> 00:42:06.110
cleaning the data, data
engineering, modeling,

00:42:06.110 --> 00:42:09.150
and putting protection,
for example.

00:42:09.150 --> 00:42:10.940
DAVID KEYES: Can I
also just give a pitch

00:42:10.940 --> 00:42:13.752
for the idea of potentially
considering consulting,

00:42:13.752 --> 00:42:15.710
because I think it's a
really good way that you

00:42:15.710 --> 00:42:18.250
can gain experience.

00:42:18.250 --> 00:42:20.810
And even if your
ultimate goal is

00:42:20.810 --> 00:42:22.220
to become, say,
a data scientist,

00:42:22.220 --> 00:42:23.887
I think there are a
lot of opportunities

00:42:23.887 --> 00:42:28.210
to use the types of skills
that you have as an R user

00:42:28.210 --> 00:42:32.450
outside of that title
in ways that can really

00:42:32.450 --> 00:42:34.580
be impactful for
the organizations

00:42:34.580 --> 00:42:35.660
that you work for.

00:42:35.660 --> 00:42:39.650
I mean, just as a
minor example, one

00:42:39.650 --> 00:42:44.000
of the clients that I was
training recently was working,

00:42:44.000 --> 00:42:46.733
doing an evaluation of
an after-school program.

00:42:46.733 --> 00:42:48.650
And they were working
at 100 different schools

00:42:48.650 --> 00:42:51.560
and they had to produce
literally 100 reports.

00:42:51.560 --> 00:42:53.480
I taught them about
parameterized reporting

00:42:53.480 --> 00:42:56.360
and it just completely blew
their mind, the idea that they

00:42:56.360 --> 00:42:59.030
could automate that and not
have to manually produce

00:42:59.030 --> 00:43:00.360
100 reports.

00:43:00.360 --> 00:43:02.030
They're not a data science firm.

00:43:02.030 --> 00:43:04.280
That's not how they
identify themselves.

00:43:04.280 --> 00:43:07.130
But those types of skills
can be really useful

00:43:07.130 --> 00:43:10.520
and serve a wide variety
of organizations.

00:43:10.520 --> 00:43:12.987
And you can do that
sometimes through consulting.

00:43:12.987 --> 00:43:15.320
JEN HECHT: I actually have a
follow-up question directed

00:43:15.320 --> 00:43:17.750
to you, David, on that
point, which is, how did you

00:43:17.750 --> 00:43:19.557
get started consulting?

00:43:19.557 --> 00:43:21.140
How did you start
out finding clients?

00:43:21.140 --> 00:43:24.200
And how has that
changed over time?

00:43:24.200 --> 00:43:26.570
DAVID KEYES: So I
started out consulting--

00:43:26.570 --> 00:43:29.180
the honest answer is
because I had twins

00:43:29.180 --> 00:43:31.850
three and 1/2 years ago and
I wanted more flexibility

00:43:31.850 --> 00:43:35.180
than in the job that
I had at the time.

00:43:35.180 --> 00:43:37.310
I started out finding clients--

00:43:37.310 --> 00:43:40.090
I've mostly done
online marketing.

00:43:40.090 --> 00:43:45.020
I offer online courses as well
as trainings for organizations.

00:43:45.020 --> 00:43:47.810
And I've tried to
basically become

00:43:47.810 --> 00:43:49.715
a resource for other folks.

00:43:49.715 --> 00:43:51.590
So I write a lot of blog
posts really pitched

00:43:51.590 --> 00:43:55.640
at newcomers learning
about packages to make

00:43:55.640 --> 00:43:57.590
effective tables or
that type of thing.

00:43:57.590 --> 00:43:59.780
And that's really been my
most effective strategy

00:43:59.780 --> 00:44:01.390
to reach out to clients.

00:44:01.390 --> 00:44:02.390
JEN HECHT: That's great.

00:44:02.390 --> 00:44:04.760
So I think we're right at time.

00:44:04.760 --> 00:44:07.360
So I'd love to give a round
of applause to the panelists.

00:44:07.360 --> 00:44:09.760
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:09.760 --> 00:44:12.760
Thank you.

00:44:12.760 --> 00:44:14.910
[CHIME RINGING]

